{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויהי בימי", :translit "vayehi bimei", :gloss "and it was in the days of"} {:he "ה' דסמיכי", :translit "heh disemachi", :gloss "five with simanim"} {:he "הכא ואידך", :translit "haka ve'idach", :gloss "here and the other"} {:he "ויהי בימי שפוט השופטים", :translit "vayehi bimei shfot hashofetim", :gloss "and it was in the days of the judging of the judges"} {:he "ויהי בימי אחשורוש", :translit "vayehi bimei achashverosh", :gloss "and it was in the days of Ahasuerus"} {:he "כו' כראיתא במדרש", :translit "kevitchra'ita bamidrash", :gloss "etc. as seen in the midrash"} {:he "כולם היתה בימיהם ווי", :translit "kulam hayta biyemeihem vav", :gloss "all of them were in their days woe"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 5, :occurrences ["ויהי בימי" "ויהי בימי שפוט השופטים" "ויהי בימי אחשורוש"]} {:kind :other, :note "He abbreviates the full list with the Aramaic mnemonic 'five with simanim' rather than enumerating all five."}], :frame {:text "The word ויהי (vayehi, 'and it was') is traditionally read as a portent of distress, as taught in Megillah 10b: 'Rav said: wherever vayehi is stated, it connotes suffering.' The Midrash on this verse (Bereishit Rabbah 42:3) lists the occurrences of 'ויהי בימי' and applies this principle: in each case the phrase introduces a period of travail — famine in Abraham's time, famine in Ruth's days, and the peril of annihilation in Esther's days. The Ba'al HaTurim condenses this midrashic list into the Aramaic mnemonic 'five with simanim.'", :sources ["Megillah 10b" "Bereishit Rabbah 42:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 5, :occurrences ["ויהי בימי" "ויהי בימי שפוט השופטים" "ויהי בימי אחשורוש"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ויהי בימי", :refs ["Genesis 14:1" "Isaiah 7:1" "Jeremiah 1:3" "Ruth 1:1" "Esther 1:1"], :n 5, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ויהי בימי שפוט השופטים", :refs ["Ruth 1:1"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ויהי בימי אחשורוש", :refs ["Esther 1:1"], :n 1, :lens :